  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe main concerns for the forecast period are dry afternoon RH\nvalues today and tomorrow, and the eventual return of rain chances\nto the area this weekend.\n\nA strong and resilient upper ridge will remain anchored over the\ncentral Gulf coast for the next 24 to 36 hours or so, before\nweakening and being shunted further south into the Gulf and\nperhaps Florida to some extent by shortwaves ejecting eastward \nfrom the Pacific NW and a large scale trough moving through \neastern Canada.\n\nRegarding the afternoon relative humidity values, NBM guidance\ncontinues to be too high with afternoon dewpoints. Blended in a\nhealthy dose of CONSShort guidance into this afternoon, and\ntomorrow's, dewpoint forecasts. The result is forecast MinRH\nvalues in the 35-45 percent range across the CWA which seems\nreasonable. Fire weather concerns remain low however, despite the\nlow RH values, as high pressure and light winds will remain in\nplace. I wouldn't expect drastically different conditions on\nWednesday either, but with the ridge breaking down and slightly\nhigher PWATs in place the afternoon RH should be a touch higher.\n\nAttention then turns to this weekend. On Friday a fairly potent\nshortwave will begin to lift NE out of Texas and move into\nKentucky and the lower Ohio valley during the weekend. While the\namplitude of the upper ridge will have been knocked down by that\ntime, we'll still have surface and H85 ridging in place as this \nsystem approaches. Given a cutoff low remaining over the Pac NW \nand a slow moving subtropical jet off the coast of the Baja, I\nwould expect that timing will continue to be the main source of\nuncertainty here and that the eventual solution will be slower\nthan at least some deterministic guidance shows. For example, the\nGFS shows rain chances returning to the area by Sat afternoon \nwhile the ECMWF keeps us dry into Sunday before rain returns. AI \nguidance seems to side with the drier side of the deterministic \nruns, and I would tend to lean that direction as well. Sunday\nseems much more likely to be when widespread rain finally moves \nback in.",
